---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import FormattedDate from '../components/FormattedDate.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import ResponsiveImage from '../components/ResponsiveImage.astro';
import { SITE_DESCRIPTION, SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';

const posts = (await getCollection('blog')).sort(
	(a, b) => b.data.pubDate.valueOf() - a.data.pubDate.valueOf(),
);
const latest = posts.slice(0, 6);

const categories = [
	{
		name: 'Missions',
		desc: 'Launches, landings & deep-space missions',
		img: '1517976487492-5750f3195933',
	},
	{
		name: 'Technology',
		desc: 'Rockets, satellites & instruments',
		img: '1446776811953-b23d57bd21aa',
	},
	{
		name: 'Astronomy',
		desc: 'Stars, galaxies & cosmic phenomena',
		img: '1462331940025-496dfbfc7564',
	},
	{
		name: 'Exploration',
		desc: 'Humanity’s quest beyond Earth',
		img: '1419242902214-272b3f66ee7a',
	},
];

const topics = [
	{
		icon: '🚀',
		title: 'Rocket Launches',
		desc: 'From liftoff to orbit — how modern launch vehicles work and where they’re headed next.',
	},
	{
		icon: '🛰️',
		title: 'Satellites & Spacecraft',
		desc: 'The machines watching Earth and voyaging outward, from CubeSats to interplanetary probes.',
	},
	{
		icon: '🔭',
		title: 'Deep-Space Science',
		desc: 'Telescopes, exoplanets, black holes — what we’re learning about the universe.',
	},
	{
		icon: '🧑‍🚀',
		title: 'Human Spaceflight',
		desc: 'Astronauts, stations, and the road back to the Moon — and on to Mars.',
	},
];

const thumbs = [
	'1447433589675-4aaa569f3e05',
	'1502134249126-9f3755a50d78',
	'1459908676235-d5f02a50184b',
	'1516849841032-87cbac4d88f7',
	'1614313913007-2b4ae8ce32d6',
	'1465101162946-4377e57745c3',
];

const faqs = [
	{
		q: 'How often do rockets launch?',
		a: 'Far more often than most people realise. The world attempted more than 250 orbital launches in 2024 alone, roughly double the busiest years of the Apollo era, and the cadence has kept climbing since. Reusable first stages are the main reason: a booster that lands and flies again turns launch from a bespoke national event into something closer to an airline operation. On an average week there are now several orbital launches worldwide, from the United States, China, Europe, India, Japan, and New Zealand, and SpaceNews covers the significant ones with previews before liftoff and verified results afterwards.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What is the International Space Station?',
		a: 'The International Space Station is a football-field-sized research laboratory orbiting Earth at about 400 kilometres altitude, travelling at 7.66 kilometres per second and completing an orbit roughly every 93 minutes. It has been continuously inhabited since November 2000 and is operated by five space agencies: NASA, Roscosmos, ESA, JAXA, and the Canadian Space Agency. Crews aboard conduct experiments in biology, materials science, and human physiology that cannot be done on Earth. The station is planned to operate until around 2030, after which commercial stations are expected to take over as the main destinations in low Earth orbit.',
	},
	{
		q: 'When are astronauts going back to the Moon?',
		a: 'NASA\u2019s Artemis programme is returning astronauts to the Moon for the first time since Apollo 17 in 1972. The plan unfolds in stages: an uncrewed test flight has already flown, a crewed flight loops around the Moon next, and a crewed landing near the lunar south pole follows, targeting the permanently shadowed craters where water ice is stored. Schedules have slipped before and may slip again, which is why SpaceNews reports target dates as targets, not promises, and updates them whenever NASA does. China has announced its own goal of landing astronauts on the Moon before 2030, so the decade ahead is shaping up as a second great era of lunar exploration.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What is a satellite mega-constellation?',
		a: 'A mega-constellation is a network of hundreds or thousands of satellites working together in low Earth orbit, instead of a single large spacecraft in geostationary orbit. SpaceX\u2019s Starlink is the largest example, delivering broadband internet from orbit, and China\u2019s GuoWang network and Amazon\u2019s Kuiper system are being built to join it. Because the satellites are close to Earth, signals travel quickly, but thousands of them are needed to cover the whole planet continuously. These systems are transforming global connectivity, and they also raise real questions about orbital debris, collision avoidance, and the brightness of satellites in the night sky, all of which SpaceNews covers as part of the story.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Why do launches get delayed so often?',
		a: 'A scrubbed launch is not a failed launch. Countdowns stop for weather, for a sensor reading a fraction out of limits, for range safety, or for a valve that needs one more inspection, and any of those can push a flight by a day or by months. Rockets leave Earth on narrow launch windows because the target orbit, the lighting at the destination, and the rotation of the Earth all have to line up. SpaceNews treats every published window as provisional, re-verifies it before each result article, and reports delays with the reason given by the operator rather than guessing. If a mission matters, the wait is part of the story.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What is space debris, and should we worry about it?',
		a: 'Space debris is everything humans have left in orbit that no longer works: dead satellites, spent rocket stages, and fragments from collisions and explosions. Tracking networks catalogue tens of thousands of objects larger than about ten centimetres, and statistical models suggest far more smaller pieces, each travelling fast enough that a paint fleck can damage a spacecraft. The risk is managed, not eliminated: satellites manoeuvre out of the way, new rules require dead spacecraft to leave busy orbits within a few years, and the first removal and servicing missions are being tested. SpaceNews covers debris as part of the infrastructure story, because a sustainable orbit is one that stays usable.',
	},
	{
		q: 'How does SpaceNews verify a launch result?',
		a: 'Liftoff is only the beginning of a launch story. SpaceNews separates three different events that are often blurred together: the rocket leaving the pad, the payload reaching orbit, and the payload being confirmed deployed and healthy. A result article is only written as a result when the operator or an official source has confirmed the relevant milestone; for classified missions, where deployment is never publicly confirmed, we say so plainly and label analyst assessments as assessments. Every article lists its sources, and our full method is published on our editorial policy page for anyone to read.',
	},
];
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title="SpaceNews | Daily Space News, Missions & Astronomy" description={SITE_DESCRIPTION} />
		<script
			type="application/ld+json"
			set:html={JSON.stringify({
				'@context': 'https://schema.org',
				'@type': 'WebSite',
				name: SITE_TITLE,
				url: 'https://SpaceNews.biz.id/',
				description: SITE_DESCRIPTION,
			})}
		/>
		<script
			type="application/ld+json"
			set:html={JSON.stringify({
				'@context': 'https://schema.org',
				'@type': 'FAQPage',
				mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
					'@type': 'Question',
					name: f.q,
					acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
				})),
			})}
		/>
		<style>
			.hero {
				text-align: center;
				padding: 6em 1.5em 5em;
			}
			.hero-inner {
				width: 860px;
				max-width: 100%;
				margin: 0 auto;
			}
			.hero h1 {
				font-size: 3.6em;
				margin-bottom: 0.4em;
			}
			.hero p.lead {
				font-size: 1.2rem;
				max-width: 62ch;
				margin: 0 auto 2.2em;
			}
			.stats {
				display: flex;
				justify-content: center;
				gap: 3em;
				flex-wrap: wrap;
				margin-bottom: 2.5em;
			}
			.stat strong {
				display: block;
				font-size: 1.9em;
				color: var(--accent);
				line-height: 1.1;
			}
			.stat span {
				font-size: 0.9rem;
				color: var(--muted);
				text-transform: uppercase;
				letter-spacing: 0.12em;
			}
			.hero-cta {
				display: flex;
				gap: 1em;
				justify-content: center;
				flex-wrap: wrap;
			}

			.cat-grid {
				display: grid;
				grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
				gap: 1.25rem;
			}
			.cat-card {
				position: relative;
				display: block;
				border-radius: var(--radius);
				overflow: hidden;
				border: 1px solid var(--border);
				aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
				transition:
					transform 0.2s ease,
					border-color 0.2s ease;
			}
			.cat-card:hover {
				transform: translateY(-4px);
				border-color: rgba(255, 176, 32, 0.5);
			}
			.cat-card img {
				position: absolute;
				inset: 0;
				width: 100%;
				height: 100%;
				object-fit: cover;
				border-radius: 0;
			}
			.cat-card::after {
				content: '';
				position: absolute;
				inset: 0;
				background: linear-gradient(to top, rgba(3, 6, 12, 0.92) 0%, rgba(3, 6, 12, 0.15) 60%);
			}
			.cat-label {
				position: absolute;
				left: 1.1em;
				right: 1.1em;
				bottom: 1.1em;
				z-index: 1;
			}
			.cat-label strong {
				display: block;
				color: #fff;
				font-size: 1.25rem;
			}
			.cat-label span {
				color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.75);
				font-size: 0.85rem;
			}

			.topic-grid {
				display: grid;
				grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
				gap: 1.25rem;
			}
			.topic-card {
				padding: 1.75em;
			}
			.topic-card .icon {
				font-size: 2rem;
			}
			.topic-card h3 {
				margin: 0.6em 0 0.4em;
			}

			.article-grid {
				display: grid;
				grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
				gap: 1.25rem;
			}
			.article-card .date {
				font-size: 0.82rem;
				color: var(--muted);
				text-transform: uppercase;
				letter-spacing: 0.1em;
				margin-bottom: 0.5em;
			}
			.article-card h3 {
				line-height: 1.3;
			}
			.article-card h3 a {
				color: var(--text);
			}
			.article-card h3 a:hover {
				color: var(--accent);
			}

			.cta-band {
				background:
					radial-gradient(800px 300px at 50% 0%, rgba(255, 176, 32, 0.1), transparent),
					var(--bg-soft);
				border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
				border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
				text-align: center;
			}
			.cta-band h2 {
				font-size: 2.6em;
				margin-bottom: 0.35em;
			}
			.cta-band p {
				max-width: 60ch;
				margin: 0 auto 2em;
				font-size: 1.1rem;
			}

			.prose {
				max-width: 76ch;
				margin: 0 auto;
			}
			.prose p {
				margin-bottom: 1.15em;
				line-height: 1.8;
				font-size: 1.05rem;
			}
			.prose p:last-child {
				margin-bottom: 0;
			}
			.prose a {
				color: var(--accent);
			}

			.deep-grid {
				display: grid;
				grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
				gap: 2.5rem 3rem;
			}
			.deep-block h3 {
				margin-bottom: 0.5em;
			}
			.deep-block p {
				line-height: 1.75;
				margin-bottom: 1em;
			}
			.deep-block p:last-child {
				margin-bottom: 0;
			}

			.matter-grid {
				display: grid;
				grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
				gap: 1.25rem;
				margin-top: 2.5rem;
			}
			.matter-card {
				padding: 1.75em;
			}
			.matter-card h3 {
				margin: 0 0 0.4em;
			}
			.matter-card p {
				line-height: 1.7;
			}

			.faq-list {
				max-width: 820px;
				margin: 0 auto;
				display: grid;
				gap: 1.25rem;
			}
			.faq-item {
				padding: 1.75em;
			}
			.faq-item h3 {
				margin: 0 0 0.5em;
			}
			.faq-item p {
				line-height: 1.75;
				margin: 0;
			}

			@media (max-width: 900px) {
				.cat-grid {
					grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
				}
				.article-grid {
					grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
				}
			}
			@media (max-width: 720px) {
				.hero {
					padding: 4em 1.25em 3.5em;
				}
				.hero h1 {
					font-size: 2.4em;
				}
				.stats {
					gap: 1.5em;
				}
				.topic-grid {
					grid-template-columns: 1fr;
				}
				.deep-grid {
					grid-template-columns: 1fr;
				}
				.matter-grid {
					grid-template-columns: 1fr;
				}
				.article-grid {
					grid-template-columns: 1fr;
				}
				.cat-card {
					aspect-ratio: 16 / 10;
				}
			}
		</style>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />

		<main>
		<section class="hero stars">
			<div class="hero-inner">
				<span class="eyebrow">Your Space News Authority</span>
				<h1>Explore the <em>Infinite</em> Universe</h1>
				<p class="lead">
					{SITE_DESCRIPTION} From rocket launches to deep-space discoveries — everything
					you need to follow humanity’s journey to the stars.
				</p>
				<div class="stats">
					<div class="stat"><strong>{posts.length}+</strong><span>Expert Articles</span></div>
					<div class="stat"><strong>4</strong><span>Topic Categories</span></div>
					<div class="stat"><strong>∞</strong><span>Space to Explore</span></div>
				</div>
				<div class="hero-cta">
					<a class="btn btn-primary" href="/blog">Start Reading</a>
					<a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/about">About Us</a>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section">
			<div class="section-inner">
				<div class="section-head">
					<span class="eyebrow">Browse by Category</span>
					<h2>Find Your <em>Orbit</em></h2>
					<p>
						Jump straight into the corner of space that fascinates you most. Each category gathers the
						coverage from its beat in one place, so a mission or a discovery you follow never goes cold
						between editions.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="cat-grid">
					{
						categories.map((c) => (
							<a class="cat-card" href="/blog">
								<ResponsiveImage id={c.img} alt={`${c.name}: ${c.desc}`} loading="lazy" />
								<span class="cat-label">
									<strong>{c.name}</strong>
									<span>{c.desc}</span>
								</span>
							</a>
						))
					}
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section" style="background: var(--bg-soft); border-top: 1px solid var(--border); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
			<div class="section-inner">
				<div class="section-head">
					<span class="eyebrow">The Big Picture</span>
					<h2>We Are Living Through a <em>New Space Age</em></h2>
				</div>
				<div class="prose">
					<p>
						Something fundamental has changed in humanity's relationship with space. A generation ago, a rocket
						launch was a rare national occasion, announced weeks ahead and watched by the whole world. Today,
						orbital launches happen several times a week, every week, from spaceports on four continents. The
						world attempted more than 250 orbital launches in 2024, roughly double the busiest years of the
						Apollo era, and the pace has not slowed since. The reason is not a single breakthrough but a stack
						of them: reusable first stages that fly home and land themselves, mass-produced satellites built on
						assembly lines instead of in clean-room cathedrals, and launch vehicles designed from the start to
						fly often and cheaply. Space has become infrastructure, and infrastructure rewards those who pay
						attention early.
					</p>
					<p>
						Nowhere is the change more visible than in the return to the Moon. NASA's Artemis programme is
						bringing astronauts back to lunar space for the first time since Apollo 17 in 1972, this time to
						stay: the long-term goal is a sustained presence around the lunar south pole, where craters in
						permanent shadow hold water ice that can become drinking water, oxygen, and even rocket propellant.
						America is not going alone, and it is not going unchallenged. China has landed a series of
						increasingly ambitious robotic missions, including the first sample return from the Moon's far side,
						and has announced its intention to land its own astronauts before 2030. India has landed near the
						south pole, Japan has put a lander down with pinpoint precision, and commercial companies are
						delivering cargo to the surface under NASA contracts. The Moon has become the proving ground where
						the next fifty years of exploration will be rehearsed.
					</p>
					<p>
						Low Earth orbit, meanwhile, is turning into an economy. Fleets of thousands of satellites, led by
						Starlink and now joined by China's GuoWang network and others, are beaming broadband to ships,
						aircraft, and villages that fibre will never reach. The International Space Station, continuously
						inhabited since November 2000, is planned to retire around 2030, and commercial stations are being
						built to take its place as laboratories, film sets, and destinations for private astronauts.
						Companies are testing in-orbit manufacturing, satellite servicing, and debris removal, because an
						orbit full of working hardware is an orbit worth maintaining. Every one of these ventures can fail,
						and some of them will; the value of honest coverage is knowing which milestones were actually
						reached and which were only announced.
					</p>
					<p>
						Above the traffic, the great observatories are rewriting astronomy. The James Webb Space Telescope,
						parked 1.5 million kilometres from Earth at the Sun–Earth L2 point, is dissecting the atmospheres
						of planets around other stars and finding mature galaxies far earlier in cosmic history than
						theories predicted. Hubble, launched in 1990, still works alongside it. On the ground, a new
						generation of extremely large telescopes is rising in the Chilean Andes, with mirrors tens of metres
						across. Robotic explorers are spread across the solar system: a spacecraft launched in 2024 is
						cruising toward Jupiter's ocean moon Europa, rovers are caching samples on Mars for a future return,
						and asteroid missions have already delivered pieces of ancient worlds to laboratories on Earth.
						This is the richest era of discovery since the first telescopes were pointed at the sky, and most of
						it happens in announcements, data releases, and mission updates that deserve more than a headline.
					</p>
					<p>
						The economics explain the momentum. Industry estimates value the global space economy at more than
						$600 billion a year, and the fastest growth is no longer in government programmes alone but in
						commercial services: broadband constellations, Earth-imaging subscriptions, rideshare launches that
						let a university fly a satellite for the price of a house. Lower launch costs compound through
						everything built on top of them. A sensor that would once have waited a decade for a bespoke ride
						to orbit now flies within a year, which means more data, more science, and more companies whose
						business plans begin with the words "in orbit". Covering that economy honestly, separating the
						manifests that flew from the decks that were only presented, is part of the same job.
					</p>
					<p>
						That is why SpaceNews exists. We publish five editions a day, every day, following launches as they
						happen and discoveries as they are confirmed, and we hold ourselves to a simple standard: liftoff
						is not deployment, a target date is not a promise, and a claim is not a fact until a source we can
						name has confirmed it. Whether you are a lifelong space enthusiast, an investor trying to
						understand the fastest-growing sector of the aerospace industry, or a student deciding whether the
						future might have a place for you in it, this is where the new space age gets documented, one
						verified story at a time.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section">
			<div class="section-inner">
				<div class="section-head">
					<span class="eyebrow">Every Angle Covered</span>
					<h2>Space, From Every <em>Angle</em></h2>
				</div>
				<div class="topic-grid">
					{
						topics.map((t) => (
							<div class="card topic-card">
								<span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">{t.icon}</span>
								<h3>{t.title}</h3>
								<p>{t.desc}</p>
								<a class="card-link" href="/blog">
									Explore Category <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
								</a>
							</div>
						))
					}
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section" style="background: var(--bg-soft); border-top: 1px solid var(--border); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
			<div class="section-inner">
				<div class="section-head">
					<span class="eyebrow">Inside Our Coverage</span>
					<h2>Four Beats, One <em>Standard</em></h2>
					<p>Every SpaceNews story belongs to one of four beats. Here is what each one actually involves.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="deep-grid">
					<div class="deep-block">
						<h3>Missions: From Countdown to Confirmed Result</h3>
						<p>
							Mission coverage is the heartbeat of SpaceNews. Before a launch, our previews lay out the window
							in plain language: when it opens and closes, which booster and spacecraft are flying and how many
							times they have flown before, what the payload is, and where the booster will try to land. After
							liftoff, we apply the distinction that matters most and is most often skipped: a rocket leaving
							the pad is not the same as a satellite reaching its orbit, and neither is the same as that
							satellite being confirmed deployed and alive. Deployment is only reported as fact when the
							operator or an official source confirms it, and for classified missions, where that confirmation
							never comes, we say exactly that.
						</p>
						<p>
							The same discipline applies between launches. Cruise phases, flybys, dockings, spacewalks, and
							landings each get covered as dated milestones on a mission timeline, so a reader who arrives
							halfway through a two-year voyage can reconstruct the whole journey. When something goes wrong,
							and in spaceflight things do, anomalies are reported with the operator's own words and the
							evidence available, never with speculation dressed up as certainty.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="deep-block">
						<h3>Technology: The Machines Behind the Headlines</h3>
						<p>
							Every mission rides on technology, and the Technology beat explains the machines without the
							marketing. Reusability is the defining engineering story of this decade: first stages that land
							on drone ships and concrete pads, fairings fished from the ocean, and now fully reusable systems
							designed to catch both booster and ship out of the air. We cover the propulsion behind it, from
							methane-burning engines built for rapid reuse to the electric thrusters that nudge satellites
							around orbit, and we report specifications from the manufacturer or agency that built the
							hardware, not from render films.
						</p>
						<p>
							The beat also follows the quieter revolutions: satellites built by the hundreds on production
							lines, in-orbit refuelling and servicing, new spacesuits and life-support systems, and the
							instruments that turn raw photons into publishable science. When a company announces a
							breakthrough, we ask the unfashionable questions: Has it flown? Has it been tested at full scale?
							Who verified it? Technology coverage earns its readers by treating engineering claims as claims
							until hardware proves them.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="deep-block">
						<h3>Astronomy: Reading the Universe Carefully</h3>
						<p>
							Astronomy moves at the speed of peer review, and our coverage respects that. Exoplanet stories,
							the most popular in this beat, rest on distinct techniques that measure different things: a
							transit records a planet's size, a radial-velocity wobble weighs it, a secondary eclipse can take
							its temperature, and direct imaging photographs the planet itself. Our articles name the
							technique, the telescope, and the team, and they report uncertainties the way the papers do,
							because a temperature with an error bar is science and a temperature without one is advertising.
						</p>
						<p>
							Beyond exoplanets, the beat spans black holes caught feeding and flaring, galaxies that challenge
							cosmology's timeline, supernovae and other transient events that fade within days, and the
							standing puzzles of the field, from the Hubble tension to the nature of dark matter. Findings are
							traced to the journal paper or observatory release behind them, linked in every article's Sources
							section, so any reader can go one level deeper than we did.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="deep-block">
						<h3>Exploration: The Human Story, Told Honestly</h3>
						<p>
							Exploration is where spaceflight becomes a human story. This beat follows astronauts and
							cosmonauts through training, launch, spacewalks, and the long, ordinary, extraordinary days of
							life aboard a station: the maintenance that keeps a laboratory alive, the experiments that only
							work in weightlessness, and the handovers between crews from different nations sharing one
							orbiting home. Commercial crew vehicles and private missions are widening who gets to fly, and we
							cover that widening as it happens, with the safety record stated as plainly as the spectacle.
						</p>
						<p>
							Looking outward, Exploration tracks the roadmaps that decide where humans go next: the Artemis
							campaign building toward a sustained lunar presence, the robotic scouts preparing the way, and
							the Mars ambitions that every lunar system is ultimately rehearsing for. Roadmaps are reported
							as roadmaps. Schedules in human spaceflight slip for honourable reasons, usually safety, and our
							coverage treats a delay as information, not failure, while never letting a rendering substitute
							for a result.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section">
			<div class="section-inner">
				<div class="section-head">
					<span class="eyebrow">Fresh Off the Press</span>
					<h2>Latest <em>Articles</em></h2>
				</div>
				<div class="article-grid">
					{
						latest.map((post, i) => (
							<article class="card article-card">
								<a href={`/blog/${post.id}/`} aria-label={post.data.title}>
									<ResponsiveImage id={thumbs[i % thumbs.length]} alt={post.data.title} loading="lazy" />
								</a>
								<div class="card-body">
									<div class="date">
										<FormattedDate date={post.data.pubDate} />
									</div>
									<h3>
										<a href={`/blog/${post.id}/`}>{post.data.title}</a>
									</h3>
									<p>{post.data.description}</p>
								</div>
							</article>
						))
					}
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section" style="background: var(--bg-soft); border-top: 1px solid var(--border); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
			<div class="section-inner">
				<div class="section-head">
					<span class="eyebrow">Down to Earth</span>
					<h2>Why Space <em>Matters</em></h2>
					<p>
						Space can look like a luxury until you notice how much of ordinary life already runs through
						orbit. These are the four services the modern world quietly depends on.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="matter-grid" style="margin-top: 0;">
					<div class="card matter-card">
						<h3>Watching the Earth</h3>
						<p>
							Weather satellites track storms days before landfall, Earth-observation fleets map wildfires,
							floods, crops, and ice in near real time, and climate records stretching back decades are the
							evidence base for everything humanity knows about its changing planet. None of that data exists
							without launches.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card matter-card">
						<h3>Position and Timing</h3>
						<p>
							Navigation constellations do far more than point drivers at destinations. Their atomic-clock
							signals timestamp financial transactions, synchronise power grids and mobile networks, and
							guide aircraft, ships, and precision farming. A world without satellite timing would notice
							within hours.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card matter-card">
						<h3>Connection Everywhere</h3>
						<p>
							Communications satellites link the places cables cannot reach: ocean-going ships, polar
							stations, disaster zones with their ground networks destroyed, and rural communities beyond
							the fibre map. Mega-constellations are now turning that emergency capability into everyday
							broadband.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card matter-card">
						<h3>Science That Spills Over</h3>
						<p>
							Space programmes push technology that returns to Earth: advanced materials, medical imaging,
							water purification, and the miniaturised sensors in every phone camera all carry spaceflight
							ancestry. And the pure science, the exoplanets and early galaxies, answers the oldest questions
							our species has asked.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="prose" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
					<p>
						None of these services arrived by accident. Each rests on decades of launches that had to work,
						satellites that had to survive, and ground systems that had to be maintained long after the
						headlines faded. That is the deeper reason launch coverage matters: every successful mission is a
						small addition to the machinery modern civilisation already runs on, and every failure is a
						reminder of how hard-won that machinery is. Following space is not an escape from Earth. Done
						properly, it is a way of understanding Earth better.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section">
			<div class="section-inner">
				<div class="section-head">
					<span class="eyebrow">Our Method</span>
					<h2>How SpaceNews <em>Covers</em> Space</h2>
				</div>
				<div class="prose">
					<p>
						Every SpaceNews article starts from primary sources: the space agency, launch provider, observatory,
						or research team behind the story. Official mission pages, agency releases, and peer-reviewed papers
						sit at the top of our sourcing hierarchy, followed by reputable specialist reporting, and every
						article ends with a Sources section linking to what was actually consulted, so the chain of
						evidence is visible rather than implied. Figures, dates, and quotations are checked against those
						sources before publication, and anything that cannot be verified is either left out or labelled
						with its true status: a target, an estimate, or an analyst's assessment.
					</p>
					<p>
						Launch coverage follows stricter rules still, because launches are where space reporting most often
						goes wrong. Windows are re-verified before every preview and every result article, since schedules
						move by hours without ceremony. A result is only declared when the milestone it claims has been
						confirmed by the operator, and when confirmation is impossible, as with classified payloads whose
						deployment is never announced, the article says so in plain words. Our newsroom also uses
						AI-assisted tools in research and production, inside the human verification process described in
						our <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a>; the standards, not the tools, decide what
						gets published.
					</p>
					<p>
						The publishing rhythm matches the subject. Five editions a day, from morning to evening, means a
						launch that lifts off at dawn is reported the same morning, a result confirmed at midday is settled
						by afternoon, and a discovery published overnight is explained before the day is out. Each edition
						is checked before it ships, and each article carries its category, its sources, and its place in
						the longer story, so a reader following one mission across months can always find the thread.
					</p>
					<p>
						And when we get something wrong, the record shows it. Our <a href="/corrections/">corrections
						policy</a> explains how to report an error, what gets corrected, and how updates are marked inside
						the article itself, because a news site about science should behave the way science does: publish
						the evidence, show the method, and correct the record in the open.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section" style="background: var(--bg-soft); border-top: 1px solid var(--border); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
			<div class="section-inner">
				<div class="section-head">
					<span class="eyebrow">Good Questions</span>
					<h2>Space, <em>Explained</em></h2>
					<p>The questions readers ask us most, answered the way our articles answer everything: with the evidence first.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="faq-list">
