		<section class="section">
			<div class="section-inner">
				<div class="section-head">
					<span class="eyebrow">Never Miss a Milestone</span>
					<h2>The Rhythm of the <em>Space Calendar</em></h2>
				</div>
				<div class="prose">
					<p>
						Space news has a rhythm, and learning it makes the whole field easier to follow. Launches come
						first: they cluster inside windows dictated by orbital mechanics, so a week can hold five liftoffs
						from three continents and the next can hold none. Around the launches runs the slower calendar of
						station life: crew rotations roughly every six months, cargo ships arriving and departing,
						spacewalks scheduled to the hour and sometimes postponed to the season. Further out, planetary
						missions measure their lives in years: a probe launched today may cruise for half a decade before
						its one great day at a distant moon, and its most important photographs may arrive years after
						the team that built it has moved on.
					</p>
					<p>
						The sky itself keeps appointments too. Meteor showers return on fixed dates, the Perseids every
						August, the Geminids every December, the Leonids each November, with rates that depend on the
						Moon and the weather more than on the shower. Eclipses trace their narrow paths across the map
						years in advance. Planets reach opposition, comets round the Sun, and telescopes publish their
						seasons of data in archives anyone can open. A good space publication treats all of it as one
						continuous calendar: previews before each window so readers know what to watch for, results after
						each milestone so the record is settled, and explainers in the quiet stretches, when there is
						finally time to ask how a rocket engine works, why a telescope sees in infrared, or what a newly
						found world might actually be like. For readers who watch the sky themselves, our previews carry
						the practical details too: the local time of the peak, the Moon phase that helps or hurts, and an
						honest range for what a dark sky can deliver, never a guaranteed spectacle.
					</p>
					<p>
						That rhythm is why SpaceNews publishes five times a day rather than once. A launch window that
						opens at dawn in one hemisphere is an evening story in another; a discovery announced in a journal
						deserves the same care as a liftoff. Whenever something in the calendar fires, and something
						almost always is firing, the coverage here follows the same sequence: verify the schedule, report
						the event, confirm the result, and link the sources. New readers usually start with the latest
						articles below the hero, then settle into a favourite beat; returning readers tend to arrive with
						one question, about one mission, and leave with the answer and its evidence attached. Either way,
						bookmark the <a href="/blog">blog</a>, and the calendar will take care of the rest.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

